WEBVTT
NOTE how-deep-can-whales-dive - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.030
Nobody saw it happen: roughly three
hundred times surface pressure, in total

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:12.336
darkness.

00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:14.292
The deepest dive ever recorded by a whale
reached 2,992 metres, made by a Cuvier's

00:00:14.292 --> 00:00:16.224
beaked whale wearing a tag off southern
California, in a study published in 2014.

00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:24.667
Guinness World Records lists it as the
deepest dive by any mammal.

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:31.672
The only witness was a pressure sensor,
which logged depth and relayed summaries

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:32.811
by satellite.

00:00:32.811 --> 00:00:33.755
That settles one thing: a whale got there
and came back.

00:00:33.755 --> 00:00:35.088
It does not settle how deep a whale can
go. A record is a floor, not a ceiling.

00:00:35.088 --> 00:00:39.083
How deep can whales dive?

00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:48.264
Nobody watched that whale reach the bottom
of its dive. Nobody could.

00:00:48.264 --> 00:00:53.038
At that depth there is no sunlight, and
Cuvier's beaked whales are hard enough to

00:00:53.038 --> 00:00:57.635
find at the surface, where they spend only
short intervals between long dives.

00:00:57.635 --> 00:01:03.106
What researchers did instead was attach an
electronic tag to the animal while it

00:01:03.106 --> 00:01:07.347
breathed at the surface, and then let the
tag do the watching.

00:01:07.347 --> 00:01:08.652
The tag carried a pressure sensor.

00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:11.648
It logged pressure at regular intervals
and, over the following weeks, relayed

00:01:11.648 --> 00:01:14.029
summarised records through satellites to
researchers on shore.

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:20.264
The 2,992 metres is what that sensor
reported at the deepest point of one dive.

00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:26.291
Pressure is a very good proxy for depth,
because seawater is heavy and its weight

00:01:26.291 --> 00:01:27.779
adds up predictably.

00:01:27.779 --> 00:01:31.073
As NOAA Ocean Exploration explains,
pressure increases by roughly one

00:01:31.073 --> 00:01:33.173
atmosphere for every ten metres you
descend.

00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:39.984
So a tag that knows the pressure knows the
depth, within the accuracy of its sensor.

00:01:39.984 --> 00:01:45.634
At 2,992 metres, that arithmetic gives
roughly three hundred times the pressure

00:01:45.634 --> 00:01:46.707
at the surface.

00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:49.692
What the sensor cannot tell you is why the
whale went there, what it found, or

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:51.032
whether it could have gone further.

00:01:51.032 --> 00:01:56.435
It also helps to know that the record was
not a lucky single reading.

00:01:56.435 --> 00:02:01.435
The study tagged several whales and
followed them for weeks, logging a great

00:02:01.435 --> 00:02:02.488
many deep dives.

00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:04.099
Most of those dives were shallower than
the record.

00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:06.216
That matters, because it tells you how to
read the headline number.

00:02:06.216 --> 00:02:17.272
It is the extreme edge of a pattern, not
the typical dive.

00:02:17.272 --> 00:02:19.061
When you see a single figure attached to a
species, ask whether it is the usual

00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:20.533
behaviour or the most extreme thing ever
caught on an instrument.

00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:21.235
Here, it is clearly the second.

00:02:21.235 --> 00:02:26.104
For most of the history of whale science,
nobody could follow a diving whale.

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:35.448
Depth had to be inferred. One line of
evidence came from the seafloor itself.

00:02:35.448 --> 00:02:38.739
In the era of submarine telegraph cables,
repair crews occasionally hauled up dead

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:41.267
sperm whales tangled in cable that had
been laid in deep water.

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:47.466
Wherever the cable lay, the whale had
evidently been.

00:02:47.466 --> 00:02:52.144
Another clue came from stomach contents.

00:02:52.144 --> 00:02:54.975
Sperm whales taken by whalers were found
to contain the hard beaks of squid,

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:57.023
including kinds that live far below the
sunlit surface.

00:02:57.023 --> 00:02:58.923
If the prey lives deep, the predator must
reach it.

00:02:58.923 --> 00:03:01.014
A third method was simply patience.

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:05.136
Observers on ships timed how long whales
stayed down between breaths.

00:03:05.136 --> 00:03:15.445
Long absences suggested deep dives, but a
stopwatch measures time, not depth.

00:03:15.445 --> 00:03:16.647
A whale could spend an hour travelling
sideways.

00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:18.624
Each of these clues was real, and each
pointed downward, but none of them could

00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:19.675
put a single trustworthy number on a dive.

00:03:19.675 --> 00:03:24.555
They established that some whales go deep.
They could not say how deep.

00:03:24.555 --> 00:03:32.005
That changed when electronics became small
and robust enough to ride on a whale.

00:03:32.005 --> 00:03:38.919
Early recorders had to be recovered to
read their memory, which meant finding a

00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:42.069
small floating tag somewhere at sea.

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:46.247
Later designs could compress their records
and transmit them by satellite whenever

00:03:46.247 --> 00:03:50.222
the whale surfaced, so the data reached
researchers even if the tag itself was

00:03:50.222 --> 00:03:51.088
never seen again.

00:03:51.088 --> 00:03:53.674
It doesn't bother me that instruments
extend what we can know.

00:03:53.674 --> 00:03:56.469
Human senses are extremely limited. That's
the point of technology.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:08.453
The obvious question is how a breathing
animal survives a dive like that.

00:04:08.453 --> 00:04:10.439
The answer is not a giant lungful of air.

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:14.266
Deep-diving whales store most of their
oxygen in their blood and, above all, in

00:04:14.266 --> 00:04:17.365
their muscles, which are packed with a
protein called myoglobin.

00:04:17.365 --> 00:04:22.170
Myoglobin binds oxygen and holds it where
it will be used.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:28.549
In some diving mammals it is so
concentrated that the muscle looks very

00:04:28.549 --> 00:04:32.419
On the way down, the whale's heart slows,
and blood flow is redirected toward the

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:36.337
organs that cannot tolerate running short,
such as the brain and the heart itself,

00:04:36.337 --> 00:04:37.675
while other tissues make do.

00:04:37.675 --> 00:04:41.021
The lungs do something that sounds
alarming.

00:04:41.021 --> 00:04:46.952
As pressure rises, they compress, and the
air in them is squeezed into stiffer

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:50.451
airways where little gas exchange takes
place.

00:04:50.451 --> 00:04:51.362
That is protective.

00:04:51.362 --> 00:04:55.149
Less nitrogen dissolves into the blood
under pressure, which lowers the risk of

00:04:55.149 --> 00:04:58.889
the gas coming out of solution on the way
up, the problem human divers know as

00:04:58.889 --> 00:04:59.992
decompression sickness.

00:04:59.992 --> 00:05:04.635
Much of this physiology was worked out in
other diving mammals, such as seals, and

00:05:04.635 --> 00:05:09.277
is inferred for beaked whales, which are
far too elusive to study in a laboratory.

00:05:09.277 --> 00:05:12.851
The 2014 study reported more than depth.

00:05:12.851 --> 00:05:20.446
That same record dive lasted more than two
hours, which was also reported as a record

00:05:20.446 --> 00:05:22.680
for a mammal at the time.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.693
Depth and duration are related but not
identical.

00:05:24.693 --> 00:05:28.103
A whale's oxygen budget limits how long it
can stay down, and depth is part of that

00:05:28.103 --> 00:05:31.307
cost, because the longer the descent and
the ascent, the less time remains for

00:05:31.307 --> 00:05:32.211
hunting at the bottom.

00:05:32.211 --> 00:05:34.720
Tag records show the typical shape.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:40.240
Cuvier's beaked whales descend steeply,
spend time hunting at depth, and then

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.395
rise, often more slowly than they went
down.

00:05:43.395 --> 00:05:45.284
Other studies, using tags that record
sound, have picked up the whales'

00:05:45.284 --> 00:05:47.360
echolocation clicks during the deep part
of the dive, which is how researchers

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.851
infer that this is where the searching for
prey happens.

00:05:48.851 --> 00:05:55.260
Between deep dives, the whales make a
series of shallower ones and spend time

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.675
near the surface.

00:05:56.675 --> 00:06:03.977
Later tagging studies have recorded even
longer dives, which tells you the 2014

00:06:03.977 --> 00:06:07.859
duration was not an absolute limit either.

00:06:07.859 --> 00:06:09.480
Cuvier's beaked whale is not the only deep
diver.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.127
Sperm whales, the largest toothed whales,
hunt squid in deep water and have long

00:06:12.127 --> 00:06:13.517
been the textbook example of a deep diver.

00:06:13.517 --> 00:06:17.749
NOAA's species profiles describe both
sperm whales and beaked whales as

00:06:17.749 --> 00:06:18.941
deep-diving hunters.

00:06:18.941 --> 00:06:23.461
But published maximum depths for sperm
whales vary, and the range in the

00:06:23.461 --> 00:06:24.653
literature is wide.

00:06:24.653 --> 00:06:28.161
Some figures come from tags, some from
older acoustic tracking, and some from

00:06:28.161 --> 00:06:28.616
inference.

00:06:28.616 --> 00:06:38.061
That variation is a property of the
evidence, not of the whales.

00:06:38.061 --> 00:06:40.442
At the other end, baleen whales such as
blue and humpback whales mostly feed much

00:06:40.442 --> 00:06:42.765
shallower, filtering swarms of small
animals that gather in the upper layers of

00:06:42.765 --> 00:06:43.059
the water.

00:06:43.059 --> 00:06:50.248
Their dives are impressive as feats of
size, but they are not contenders for the

00:06:50.248 --> 00:06:51.416
depth record.

00:06:51.416 --> 00:06:52.726
Size, in other words, is not depth.

00:06:52.726 --> 00:06:55.720
The deepest known diver is a medium-sized
whale most people have never heard of.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:07.811
Here is the most important thing to
understand about any record like this.

00:07:07.811 --> 00:07:08.654
It is a lower bound.

00:07:08.654 --> 00:07:12.069
Only a small number of Cuvier's beaked
whales have ever been tagged, and each tag

00:07:12.069 --> 00:07:15.442
stays attached for a limited time, usually
days to weeks, before it falls off or

00:07:15.442 --> 00:07:16.243
stops transmitting.

00:07:16.243 --> 00:07:21.005
Each sensor also has a rated maximum depth
it can measure.

00:07:21.005 --> 00:07:27.245
A whale that dives beyond what has been
sampled leaves no trace in the data.

00:07:27.245 --> 00:07:28.589
The ocean also sets a local limit.

00:07:28.589 --> 00:07:31.551
A whale cannot dive deeper than the
seafloor beneath it, so a tagged animal

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:34.672
living over moderately deep water may
never show what it could do over a deeper

00:07:34.672 --> 00:07:34.909
basin.

00:07:34.909 --> 00:07:39.386
And there may be a physiological cost that
rises steeply with depth, so that a whale

00:07:39.386 --> 00:07:41.304
able to go deeper rarely chooses to.

00:07:41.304 --> 00:07:44.817
Researchers can describe what tagged
whales did.

00:07:44.817 --> 00:07:49.501
Describing what they could do is a
different and harder problem.

00:07:49.501 --> 00:07:53.184
Notice that every number in this episode
came from an instrument that rode along

00:07:53.184 --> 00:07:55.853
with an animal, or from arithmetic you can
check yourself.

00:07:55.853 --> 00:07:59.127
Guinness World Records lists the record
dive.

00:07:59.127 --> 00:08:03.347
NOAA Ocean Exploration explains the
pressure relationship.

00:08:03.347 --> 00:08:06.291
Smithsonian Ocean describes the deep sea
as a place sunlight does not reach, which

00:08:06.291 --> 00:08:08.195
is why no camera on a ship could have seen
that dive.

00:08:08.195 --> 00:08:14.070
None of the graphics is a picture of the
whale at the bottom.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.477
They are readings, drawn.

00:08:16.477 --> 00:08:22.678
This evidence establishes that at least
one Cuvier's beaked whale reached 2,992

00:08:22.678 --> 00:08:24.248
metres and returned.

00:08:24.248 --> 00:08:26.786
It does not establish the maximum depth
any whale can reach, the depth limit of

00:08:26.786 --> 00:08:28.200
the species, or how often such dives
happen.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:37.379
It rests on a small number of tagged
animals in a few regions.

00:08:37.379 --> 00:08:41.621
It also says nothing about what the whale
found at the bottom of that dive.

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:45.128
The sensor recorded pressure, and pressure
is all it recorded.

00:08:45.128 --> 00:08:50.093
The tag does not output depth directly; it
records pressure at set intervals.

00:08:50.093 --> 00:08:54.523
Researchers first correct the raw pressure
for water temperature and salinity,

00:08:54.523 --> 00:08:59.181
because the same pressure can correspond
to slightly different depths if the water

00:08:59.181 --> 00:09:00.373
is denser or lighter.

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:06.005
After correction, the pressure‑to‑depth
conversion (roughly 1 atm per 10 m of

00:09:06.005 --> 00:09:09.661
seawater) yields a depth value for each
timestamp.

00:09:09.661 --> 00:09:14.571
The compressed summaries sent via
satellite are then decompressed on shore,

00:09:14.571 --> 00:09:19.939
giving scientists a full dive profile that
shows descent, bottom time, and ascent.

00:09:19.939 --> 00:09:25.922
NOAA’s species profile for Cuvier’s beaked
whale reports that their typical foraging

00:09:25.922 --> 00:09:31.336
dives centre around 1 500 metres, with a
common range of 1 000–2 000 metres.

00:09:31.336 --> 00:09:36.022
This figure comes from the same tagging
efforts that produced the extreme record,

00:09:36.022 --> 00:09:40.592
showing that while individuals can reach
nearly three kilometres, most of their

00:09:40.592 --> 00:09:43.021
hunting occurs at roughly half that depth.

00:09:43.021 --> 00:09:50.312
So how deep can whales dive? At least
2,992 metres, because a tag says so.

00:09:50.312 --> 00:09:55.619
Beyond that, the honest answer is that the
ocean has not been asked often enough.

00:09:55.619 --> 00:10:00.473
Every tag that rides down with a whale is
a small question put to the dark, and each

00:10:00.473 --> 00:10:03.709
answer is only as deep as the dive it
happened to catch.
